<!--
  Which ways are roads and how fast a car drives them. One country, AAA, from 1°W to 2°E and 1°S to 3.5°N,
  and one setup per parallel, none joined to another. The origin of each setup is its westmost (or eastmost)
  node; the longest travel time from it gives maxHours.

  0°N    primary (70 km/h) with maxspeed=30, 0°–0.27°E: 30.0 km at 30 km/h, 1.20 h of travel → maxHours 2
         (at 70 km/h it would be 0.51 h → 1).
  0.5°N  secondary (60) with maxspeed=100, 0°–0.5°E: 55.6 km at 60 km/h, 1.11 h → 2 (at 100: 0.67 h → 1).
  1°N    tertiary (50) with maxspeed="20 mph" (32.2 km/h), 0°–0.3°E: 33.4 km, 1.24 h → 2 (at 50: 0.80 h → 1).
  1.5°N  tertiary 0°–0.3°E (33.3 km at 50 km/h, 0.80 h → 1), going on east as a residential road and north-east as a
         primary ferry: neither is a road of the graph.
  2°N    one-way secondary eastbound 0°–0.5°E, then two-way secondary 0.5°–0.7°E (22.2 km, 0.44 h → 1).
         From the east end the one-way road is out of reach (with it, 1.56 h → 2).
  2.5°N  motorway (one-way without a tag) 0°–0.9°E, then two-way secondary 0.9°–1.1°E (0.44 h → 1). From the
         east end the motorway is out of reach (with it, 1.53 h → 2).
  3°N    tertiary 0°–0.3°E (0.80 h → 1), going on east as a tertiary with access=no.
-->
